-- title:
--   Relative degree is preserved by constant field extension
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure, let $F_0$ be an intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$ inside the field of formal Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $t \in \mathbb{Q}((q))$ be an element lying in $F_0$ and transcendental over $\mathbb{Q}$. Write $\overline{(\cdot)}$ for the ring homomorphism `coeffEmb L` from $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ to $L((q))$ obtained by applying the structure map $\mathbb{Q} \to L$ to each coefficient of a Laurent series, and let `laurentBaseChange L F₀` be the intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$ generated over $L$ by the image $\overline{F_0}$ of $F_0$ under this map. The assertion is the equality of relative degrees
--   $$[\,\mathrm{laurentBaseChange}\ L\ F_0 : L(\overline{t})\,] = [\,F_0 : \mathbb{Q}(t)\,],$$
--   both sides being Mathlib's `IntermediateField.relfinrank` of the indicated pair of intermediate fields, the first pair inside $L((q))$ with $L(\overline{t})$ the $L$-subfield generated by $\overline{t}$, the second inside $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ with $\mathbb{Q}(t)$ the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield generated by $t$. In each pair the smaller field is contained in the larger, so each side is the degree of the extension, with the convention that it is $0$ when the extension is infinite; in particular the equality also transports finiteness of the degree in both directions.
--
--   This is the statement that the relative degree of a subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over a rational subfield $\mathbb{Q}(t)$ is unchanged under extension of the constant field from $\mathbb{Q}$ to $L$, i.e. linear disjointness of $L$ and $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ in the form of a constant field extension for function fields. It is used to carry degree computations for modular function fields, such as the degree $\ell+1$ of the extension generated by $j(\ell q)$ over the field generated by $j(q)$, from the field of definition $\mathbb{Q}$ to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ or any other coefficient field, and is invoked by the lemmas relating `relfinrank` to degrees along morphisms of curves.
-- source:
